<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lugii</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lygii</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lugii were a large group of peoples in the region of modern southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, between the Oder and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> and Ptolemy in the 1st and 2nd centuries CE. They were subject for a time to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> of the Marcomanni and in 50 CE helped overthrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannius">Vannius</a> of the Quadi. Archaeologists often link them with the Przeworsk culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lugii.jpg" alt="Lugii" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrein · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_125_(Lugii).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name is first attested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, writing in the late Augustan period, and appears later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>, Ptolemy and, last of all, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zosimus">Zosimus</a>. Tacitus treats it as a nomen, an overarching name that &quot;extends most widely&quot; and is spread across several communities, rather than the name of one tribe. No derivation has won general agreement. Günter Neumann linked it to a root meaning to swear or bind oneself by treaty, found only in Celtic and Germanic, so that the Lugii would be the members of an oath-bound league; others have proposed the Celtic god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugus">Lugus</a> or a Gaulish word for black.</p><p>Their ethnic affiliation is disputed. Most scholars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> have regarded the group as mainly Germanic with earlier Celtic elements, and Reinhard Wenskus, Klaus Tausend and Herwig Wolfram saw a union first under Celtic and later under Germanic dominance. Many connect the Lugii, wholly or in part, with the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, whose name Vandilii came to replace theirs, though Walter Pohl and Kazimierz Godłowski warned against a simple equation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> names five member tribes, saying he gives only the strongest: the Harii, the Helvecones, the Manimi, the Helisii and the Naharvali. Ptolemy lists three sub-groups along the western side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, the Omanoi, Dounoi and Bouroi; the Buri, whom he counts among the Lugii, Tacitus assigns instead to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a>. Their lands are generally placed north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetes">Sudetes</a> and the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>, perhaps as far as the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a>, with the Gotones beyond them to the north.</p><p>Among the Naharvali, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> reports, a grove of ancient cult was shown, presided over by a priest in female dress. Its twin gods, the Alci, were likened to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castor-and-pollux">Castor and Pollux</a>, and there were no images. Tausend, following Ludwig Schmidt, sees this sanctuary as the centre of the league, while Helmut Castritius doubts that the cult held the whole community together.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> calls the Lugii a large people whom the Marcomannic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> drew into his realm. In 92, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>, Lugii at war with certain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> sent envoys to the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> and received a force of one hundred horsemen, and the angered Suebi joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>. The Lugii vanish from the Greek and Roman record in the 3rd century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zosimus">Zosimus</a>, writing around 500, reports that the emperor Probus crushed the Lugii under their king Semno in a great battle; Castritius treats the notice with scepticism, while Tausend takes it as the last witness to the league.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lugii” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By Strabo's account his empire included the <b>Lugii</b>, the Semnones and peoples such as the Zumi, Butones, Mugilones and Sibini, and by 5 CE the Langobardi; Velleius said he subjugated all his neighbours by war or treaty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>There he subjected neighbouring peoples, including the Quadi, <b>Lugii</b>, Semnones and Langobardi, building a realm that stretched from the Danube to the Elbe and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-maroboduus">Kingdom of Maroboduus</a></li><li><blockquote>In 50 Vannius was overthrown by his nephews Vangio and Sido with the help of the Hermunduri and <b>Lugii</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-vannius">Kingdom of Vannius</a></li></ul></section>
